  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Minimal pre-Thanksgiving travel weather impacts \n\nWednesday will have a cold start with low temperatures this\nmorning beginning in the upper teens across the northwestern\nmountains to the mid 20s over the southeast. Strong high\npressure overhead will keep Pennsylvania dry today. The\naforementioned high pressure will gradually break down into this\nafternoon and drift north with a surface trough developing over\nthe Ohio Valley and western Pennsylvania. The resulting \npressure gradient will allow for gusty winds over the Laurel \nHighlands today with gusts between 20 and 25 mph. These winds \nwill be a precursor to a Thanksgiving day coastal storm. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Damaging winds and widespread rain for\nThanksgiving Day \n\nWidespread soaking of 1 to 2 inches of rainfall expected on \nThanksgiving Day as a very strong easterly low level flow \nassociated with a powerful coastal storm system transports \nanomalous moisture into central PA. Model consensus timing \nsuggests precip crosses the MD line around or shortly after \n12Z/7AM Thursday, but there is still some uncertainty regarding \nstart time. The heaviest rain is currently projected to fall \nbetween 7AM Thu and 1AM Fri. \n\nStrong to damaging wind gusts are expected with this system, \nparticularly over the southeastern third of the CWA and the\nLaurel Highlands. Recent runs of some of the deterministic \nmodels, including the GFS and ECMWF, have shown a 60 to 90 knot \n850 mb low-level easterly jet developing in response to the \nstrong pressure gradient between the deepening low to the south \nand retreating high pressure to the north. It remains to be seen\nhow much of this wind will be able to make it down to the \nsurface, but soundings from the GFS show 45-50 knot winds at the\ntop of the mixed layer Thursday afternoon over the Lower \nSusquehanna Valley. The RRFS soundings have shown the potential\nfor 60+ knot winds to mix down to the surface even with a weak\ntemperature inversion in place. This is likely due to just how\nstrong the pressure gradient is and the strength of the warm air\nadvection from the south.\n\nConfidence has increased in wind gusts of 60+ mph over our\neastern counties, and thus a High Wind Warning has been issued\nfor Lancaster, Lebanon, and Schuylkill counties. The Laurel \nHighlands are expected to see gusts up to 50 mph Thursday \nthrough Saturday, and there remains some thinking that a High\nWind Warning may be needed for the strongest downslope winds on\nThursday over the Laurels, but we have opted for issuing a Wind\nAdvisory for now. Elsewhere in PA gusts of 40 to 45 mph are\npossible and a Wind Advisory has been issued for most of our\ncounties where confidence in these gusts are highest. Timing of\nstrongest winds will be Thursday afternoon. Of note, the strong\nwinds on Thursday will be from the east which anecdotally does \ntend to cause more tree damage since trees are \"tested\" a lot \nless frequently from the east than from the north or west in \nthis region. Unfortunately, isolated to scattered power outages \nwill be possible on Thanksgiving, especially in the Lower Susq \nValley where winds are strongest. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: Breezy on Friday with rain showers/elevation snow \n\nColder air will gradually wrap back into Pennsylvania late \nThursday night and Friday as the system stacks up along the\nNorth-Central Appalachians. This should allow for snow to begin\nmixing in especially over the higher terrain from the Laurel\nHighlands northward along the Allegheny Plateau into northern\nPA. Rain/snow mix during the day Friday beneath the stacked low\ntransitions to more of a lake effect/upslope precip pattern \nFriday night into Saturday as the system lifts into New England.\nIt will remain breezy into the holiday weekend with west winds \ngusting 25-35 mph.",
